But behind the bright canvas of music and play,  
Was a dangerous passion that walked the wrong way.  
His Sonnets of Dark Love were hidden from view,  
The forbidden desires that everyone knew.  
He loved with a fury that bordered on fright,  
In a country of iron, of shadow, and night.

Then the civil war erupted, a furnace of hate,  
And the fascists of Franco marched into the gate.  
They hated his genius, his free-thinking art,  
And the _unnatural_ passions that lived in his heart.  
They hunted the poet through Granada’s old street,  
While the drums of the tragedy ruthlessly beat.

He refused to take flight, with his visor wide thrown,  
For he thought that his city would shelter its own.  
But they dragged him away to the olive-tree hill,  
Where the air of the morning was heavy and chill.  
By the spring of Alfacar, they ordered the slate,  
And the voice of the republic surrendered to fate.

× × ×

They shot him in darkness, they left him un-found,  
In a grave without name in the Andalusian ground.  
They killed the frail vessel, they silenced the man,  
But they couldn’t undo where the poetry began.  
The wind through the olives still carries his cry—  
The immortal Lorca, the voice of the sky.
